"Disproving Two Conjectures on the Hamiltonicity of Venn Diagrams (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol367-socg2026/LIPIcs.SoCG.2026.22/LIPIcs.SoCG.2026.22.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Figure 5 Hamilton cycles in the arrangement graphs of the Venn diagrams from Figure 1 (a)–(c). ... Figure 6 A counterexample to Pruesse and Ruskey’s conjecture for n = 4, namely a non-simple\n22:6\nDisproving Two Conjectures on the Hamiltonicity of Venn Diagrams\n1.2\nPruesse and Ruskey’s conjecture\nWhile working on Winkler’s conjecture, Pruesse and Ruskey [14] considered the question of\nfinding a Hamilton cycle not in the dual graph of a Venn diagram, but in the arrangement\ngraph whose vertices are the crossings of the curves, and curve segments between consecutive\ncrossings form the edges; see Figure 5. Note that these graphs may have multiple edges.\nFigure 5 Hamilton cycles in the arrangement graphs of the Venn diagrams from Figure 1 (a)–(c).\n▶Theorem 4 ([14, Cor. 1]). For n ≥2, the arrangement graph of any simple n-Venn diagram\nhas a Hamilton cycle.\nTheir proof demonstrates that the arrangement graph of a simple Venn diagram is\n4-connected for n ≥3, and thus a well-known theorem of Tutte [17] implies Hamiltonicity.\nPruesse and Ruskey also conjectured that their result generalizes to non-simple diagrams.\n▶Conjecture 5 ([14, Conj. 1]). For n ≥2, the arrangement graph of any (not necessarily\nsimple) n-Venn diagram has a Hamilton cycle.\n1.2.1\nThe counterexamples\nWe disprove Conjecture 5 by providing counterexamples for all n ≥4. Similarly to Theorem 2,\nour counterexamples have an even number of vertices and do not admit a perfect matching,\nwhich would be necessary for having a Hamilton cycle. One of the counterexamples for n = 4\nis shown in Figure 6.\n▶Theorem 6. For every n ≥4, there is a non-simple n-Venn diagram whose arrangement\ngraph has an even number of vertices and no perfect matching, and hence no Hamilton cycle.\nFigure 6 A counterexample to Pruesse and Ruskey’s conjecture for n = 4, namely a non-simple\n4-Venn diagram whose arrangement graph has an even number of vertices and no perfect matching,\nand hence no Hamilton cycle. The 6 red vertices form an independent set, and the complement has\nonly 4 vertices, witnessing the non-existence of a perfect matching.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCOUNTEREXAMPLES TO TWO CONJECTURES (https://tmuetze.de/papers/venn.pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; statement that the dual graph of any simple Venn diagram has a Hamilton cycle. ... While working on Winkler’s conjecture, Pruesse and Ruskey considered the (multi)graph ... display diagrams with n = 4 and n = 5 curves, respectively.\nCOUNTEREXAMPLES TO TWO CONJECTURES\nON VENN DIAGRAMS\nSOFIA BRENNER, LINDA KLEIST, TORSTEN MÜTZE, CHRISTIAN RIECK, AND FRANCESCO VERCIANI\nAbstract. In 1984, Winkler conjectured that every simple Venn diagram with n curves can\nbe extended to a simple Venn diagram with n + 1 curves. His conjecture is equivalent to the\nstatement that the dual graph of any simple Venn diagram has a Hamilton cycle. In this work,\nwe construct counterexamples to Winkler’s conjecture for all n ≥6. As part of this proof, we\ncomputed all 3.430.404 simple Venn diagrams with n = 6 curves (even their number was not\npreviously known), among which we found 72 counterexamples.\nWhile working on Winkler’s conjecture, Pruesse and Ruskey considered the (multi)graph\nwhose vertices are the crossings of the curves of a Venn diagram and curve segments between\nconsecutive crossings form the edges. They proved that this graph has a Hamilton cycle for\nevery simple Venn diagram with n curves, and conjectured that this also holds for non-simple\ndiagrams. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKOLKOM\nKOLLOQUIUM ÜBER KOMBINATORIK\n14 – 15 Novemb (https://www.kolkom.de/download/program/2025-Bielefeld-program.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Counterexamples to two conjectures on Venn diagrams ... Pruesse and Ruskey proved that this graph has a Hamilton cycle for every\nTorsten Mütze\n(Universität Kassel)\nCounterexamples to two conjectures on Venn diagrams\nIn 1984, Winkler conjectured that every simple Venn diagram with n curves\ncan be extended to a simple Venn diagram with n+1 curves. His conjecture\nis equivalent to the statement that the dual graph of any simple Venn dia-\ngram has a Hamilton cycle. In this work, we construct counterexamples to\nWinkler’s conjecture for all n >= 6. As part of this proof, we computed all\n3.430.404 simple Venn diagrams with n = 6 curves (even their number was\nnot previously known), among which we found 72 counterexamples.\nWe also disprove another conjecture about the Hamiltonicity of the (primal)\ngraph of a Venn diagram. Specifically, while working on Winkler’s conjecture,\nPruesse and Ruskey proved that this graph has a Hamilton cycle for every\nsimple Venn diagram with n curves, and conjectured that this also holds for\nnon-simple diagrams. We construct counterexamples to this conjecture for\nall n >= 4.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe also disprove another conjecture about the Hamiltonicity of the (primal) graph of a Venn diagram. Specifically, while working on Winkler’s conjecture, Pruesse and Ruskey [2] proved that this graph has a Hamilton cycle for every simple Venn diagram with n curves, and conjectured that this also holds for non-simple diagrams. We construct counterexamples to this conjecture for all n ≥ 4. (https://candc.upjs.sk/history/25abs.pdf)\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.2 years ago; Specifically, while working on Winkler’s conjecture, Pruesse and Ruskey [2] proved that this graph has a Hamilton cycle for every simple Venn diagram with n curves, and conjectured that this also holds for non-simple diagrams. ... Ruskey, All simple Venn diagrams are Hamiltonian, arXiv: 1504.06651 (2015).\nWe also disprove another conjecture about the Hamiltonicity of the (primal) graph of a Venn diagram. Specifically, while working on Winkler’s conjecture, Pruesse and Ruskey [2] proved that this graph has a Hamilton cycle for every simple Venn diagram with n curves, and conjectured that this also holds for non-simple diagrams. We construct counterexamples to this conjecture for all n ≥ 4.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nColored line drawing of a 6-Venn diagram counterexample: multiple overlapping closed curves in red, blue, green, orange, purple, and brown form a symmetric nested polygon-like arrangement with a light blue shaded central region.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 1: A counterexample to Winkler’s conjecture for n = 6, namely a simple 6-Venn diagram that cannot be extended to a simple 7-Venn diagram by adding a suitable curve.\n\nREFERENCES\n\n[1] P. Winkler, Venn diagrams: some observations and an open problem, Congr. Numer. 45 (1984), 267–274.\n\n[2] G. Pruesse, F. Ruskey, All simple Venn diagrams are Hamiltonian, arXiv: 1504.06651 (2015).\n\nEdge-uncoverability of cubic graphs\n\nMakuochukwu Felix Oguagbaka  \n(joint work with Robert Lukot’ka)\n\nAn extension of Petersen’s Perfect Matching Theorem by Schö nberger implies that every bridgeless cubic graph admits a set of perfect matchings covering all its edges. Berge conjectured that at most five perfect matchings suffice. Cubic graphs that require at least five perfect matchings to cover their edges are particularly interesting, as they could serve as counterexamples to famous conjectures, including the Cycle Double Cover Conjecture. On the other hand, cubic graphs\n\n19--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch – Joel Brewster Lewis (https://blogs.gwu.edu/jblewis/research/)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Comb